### 4. Ookla: Measuring Starlink Direct to Cell on ordinary phones in the wild
* **Speaker:** Luke Kehoe
* **Slides:** [Ookla: Measuring Starlink Direct to Cell on ordinary phones in the wild](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-maprg-ookla-measuring-starlink-direct-to-cell-on-ordinary-phones-in-the-wild-01)
* Luke Kehoe presented Ookla's analysis combining controlled drive/walk testing with crowdsourced passive telemetry to reconstruct and evaluate SpaceX's Starlink Direct-to-Cell (D2C) constellation performance on unmodified mobile devices.
* Key observations include:
  * Spatial clustering of usage in mountainous regions, national parks, and during summer months.
  * Rapid handoffs with devices connecting to a new satellite roughly every 45 seconds and new beams every 10–15 seconds.
  * Controlled testing showed an 86% SMS delivery success rate inside moving vehicles (compared to 98% on terrestrial networks), with longer transmission delays.
  * High uplink transmit power impacts battery life since phones operate at maximum power regardless of signal level.
  * Observed a 15-second reporting pattern concentrated in Samsung devices, which is under further investigation.
* Geoff Huston commented on latency jitter and packet loss during satellite handovers observed on fixed Dishy terminals, asking if alternative flow control algorithms like BBR were evaluated. Luke noted current D2C applications are low-bandwidth/emergency text-focused, with capacity traded for link reliability via lower coding rates.
* Mohit Bajpai asked about data collection ethics, user consent, and battery impact; Luke clarified data comes from foreground application sessions with explicit user opt-in and anonymized telemetry.

