### 6. TurboTest: Learning When Less Is Enough Through Early Termination of Internet Speed Tests
* **Speaker:** Arika Sharma
* **Slides:** [TurboTest: Learning When Less Is Enough Through Early Termination of Internet Speed Tests](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-maprg-turbotest-learning-when-less-is-enough-through-early-termination-of-internet-speed-tests-03)
* Arika Sharma presented TurboTest (published at NSDI 2024), a machine learning framework designed to terminate speed tests early while maintaining throughput estimation accuracy.
* Analysis of M-Lab data showed that 10% of high-throughput tests generate 46% of total transferred data.
* TurboTest uses a two-stage architecture:
  1. An XGBoost regressor predicting final throughput using kernel TCP telemetry.
  2. A transformer-based classifier determining the optimal stopping timestamp given an operator-defined error tolerance ($\epsilon$).
* Evaluation on 800k real-world tests demonstrated a data reduction of up to 92% (reducing M-Lab data volume from 12 PB to under 1 PB) within a 15% relative error margin, outperforming BBR-based heuristic stopping criteria.
* Tommy Pauly pointed to `draft-ietf-ippm-responsiveness` (work in IPPM/BPM) as another candidate framework where early termination and ML prediction could reduce network test overhead.
* Alan Frindell (via chat) and Warren Kumari expressed interest in the deployment progress; Arika noted server-side prototypes exist and a client-side implementation is under development.

